OPTIMIZATION 3: OPENMP PARALLELISM (src/program.cpp, Makefile)

WHY
- perf after Opt 2 (diabetes): execute_kernel 51%
- So formula evaluation is ~90% of runtime. Everything else (program/node copying, etc.) ~3%.
- Each program's evaluation is independent, so the outer loop over programs can run in parallel. This speeds up both the kernel and the libm calls.

CHANGE
- Added above the outer pid loop in execute_kernel:
  #pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic)
- Added -fopenmp to CXXFLAGS.
- Safe: threads only read shared input data, each has its own stack, and each writes to its own slice of y_pred. No races.


RESULTS (Opt 2 -> Opt 3, speedup; total speedup vs baseline)
- diabetes:    1.32 s -> 0.40 s (3.3x); total 51.6x
- cancer:      18.1 s -> 12.4 s (1.5x); total 10.3x
- housing:     20.5 s -> 5.87 s (3.5x); total 41.1x
- silk-wave:   31.0 s -> 13.1 s (2.4x); total 29.9x
- square-wave: 97.7 s -> 38.9 s (2.5x); total 31.1x

CORRECTNESS
- MSE output identical to Opt 2 (diff empty). Same computation, just on different cores.

OBSERVATIONS
- Diabetes also scaled well (3.3x) despite being small, so its serial part is small too.
- Cancer scaled poorly (1.5x): a large serial part outside execute_kernel. It also gained least in Opt 2.
